《具身智能发展报告（2024年，中国信通院）》和《中国人工智能系列白皮书-具身智能（2026版）》，反映具身智能在 2024–2026 年已形成系统化研究与白皮书体系；Embodied AI  
is widely defined as an intelligent form where AI is given a physical body that interacts with the real world in a perception–decision–action–re‑perception loop,
and humanoid and service robots are regarded as key carriers of embodied intelligence.。[11][12][3][4][2]                                                        

现有公开资料显示：Multiple Chinese media and technical articles describe embodied AI as a core technical route for humanoid robots, enabling them to evolve from 
executing preset motions to having perception, cognition and autonomous decision‑making in complex, unstructured environments.；Analyses of embodied AI for      
humanoid robots highlight required capabilities such as precise 3D perception in complex physical environments, task planning and execution, strong general      
knowledge, multi‑level semantic reasoning, multi‑turn spoken dialogue, and technical breakthroughs in sensor fusion, multimodal perception, world models,        
reinforcement and imitation learning, and real‑time control.；Chinese policy documents and media reports state that China has incorporated embodied intelligence 
and humanoid robots into national industrial priorities, including the 'Humanoid Robot Innovation Development Guiding Opinions', which target forming a complete 
industrial ecosystem by around 2027 and are supported by local subsidies across R&D, production and sales.；A 2025 white paper reports that the global humanoid  
robot market size exceeded roughly USD 100 billion in 2025, with China as a core growth pole; service humanoids account for about 58% and industrial humanoids   
about 37% of the market, with over 60% self‑sufficiency in key components such as motors, reducers and sensors in China and exports of these components growing  
by about 210% year‑on‑year.。[1][2][6][7][12][8][11]                                                                                                             

现有公开资料显示：Chinese market analyses estimate that the Chinese embodied intelligence market grew from about 213.3 billion RMB in 2018 to about 915 billion  
RMB in 2025 and is projected to exceed 1 trillion RMB in 2026, with 2025 financing in China’s embodied AI industry reported at around 334.73 billion RMB, roughly
four times 2024, across more than 305 deals and over 600 participating investment institutions.；Several reports and investment analyses describe 2025 as the    
'first year of mass production' for humanoid robots and project that in 2026 global humanoid shipments could exceed 50,000 units with year‑on‑year growth above  
700%.；Market forecasts cited in Chinese analyses project that global humanoid robot shipments could rise from about 25,000 units in 2024 to about 3.706 million 
units by 2030 (compound annual growth rate around 85.7%), with the humanoid robot market space increasing from around USD 50 billion in 2025 to about USD 300    
billion in 2030.；A 2025 financial article estimates that the 2025 global humanoid robot market size is about 17 billion RMB, with China at about 8.5 billion RMB
(roughly half of global), and that China’s humanoid robot shipments are around 12,000 units with year‑on‑year growth of about 420%.。[9][7][8][10]               

现有公开资料显示：Analyses of application demand report that medical care, educational companionship and logistics handling together account for over 70% of     
global humanoid robot market demand, with domestic service robot density in China around 42 units per 10,000 people, and 2025 global humanoid‑related patent     
applications rising by about 45% year‑on‑year (China about 38% of these) while global investment reaches about USD 8.7 billion focusing on embodied AI and       
flexible joint technologies.；Chinese white papers and analyses state that China is in the global first tier for embodied intelligence and humanoid robot        
industries, with domestic capabilities in key components such as motors, reducers and sensors enabling whole‑machine humanoid costs that are roughly half those  
of comparable overseas products.；Reports highlight harmonic reducers and other core components as bottlenecks for humanoid robot mass production, although      
advances in foundational algorithms and servo drive technologies are described as gradually easing these constraints.；Multiple sources identify Tesla, Unitree  
(宇树科技), Figure AI, Agility Robotics, Boston Dynamics and UBTECH (优必选) as representative global companies in embodied AI humanoid robots during the        
2024–2026 period, frequently cited as leading examples in discussions of 'the world’s strongest humanoid robots'.。[11][9][8][10][1][7]                          

现有公开资料显示：Chinese analyses frequently cite Tuosida (拓斯达), Unitree (宇树科技) and Zhiyuan Robot (智元机器人) as leading Chinese embodied‑intelligence  
humanoid robot companies, respectively emphasizing industrial mass‑production applications, high‑dynamic consumer and industrial humanoids, and a 'body+AI'      
full‑stack embodied intelligence approach.；Unitree (宇树科技) is portrayed as a leading Chinese embodied‑intelligence humanoid company focusing on high‑dynamic 
performance and both consumer and industrial humanoids: its H1 biped robot reportedly can perform backflips and is used for warehouse inspection, the G1 consumer
humanoid targets household service and educational entertainment with rising orders after a 2025 Spring Festival Gala appearance, the Tiangong Ultra system has  
attempted a half‑marathon, and Unitree released a new embodied‑intelligence robot integrating advanced joint motors and reducers in December 2025 while its joint
motors show higher torque density (about 189.5 N·m/kg) than Tesla Optimus waist joints (about 79.6 N·m/kg).；Zhiyuan Robot (智元机器人) is described as building 
a 'body+AI' full‑stack system with a self‑developed embodied intelligence large model; its Yuanzheng A series humanoids achieve dual‑arm industrial‑grade        
repeatability, use lidar‑vision fusion navigation to autonomously load/unload and maintain equipment on factory lines, and the company listed on the Hong Kong   
Stock Exchange in December 2023 and acquired A‑share firm Fenglong in 2025 with investment from firms such as BYD and Hillhouse.；Tuosida (拓斯达) is highlighted
as a benchmark for industrial application of humanoid robots in China: it self‑develops control, servo and vision technologies, uses the X5 control platform and 
an embodied‑intelligence large model in its 'Xiaotuo' humanoid, which reportedly became the first humanoid deployed in the injection‑molding industry and can    
handle over 50 industrial tasks such as palletizing, inspection and assembly, with technical specs including 21 degrees of freedom, ±0.05 mm arm repeatability,  
10 kg single‑arm payload and a payload‑to‑weight ratio around 0.67.。[8][4][10]                                                                                  

现有公开资料显示：UBTECH (优必选) and Leju Robot (乐聚机器人) are cited as Chinese humanoid companies moving into small‑batch or larger‑scale production: UBTECH 
plans to increase deliveries of its Walker humanoid and expand into industrial and service scenarios, while Leju launched its first humanoid production line on  
26 December 2024 with a designed annual capacity of about 200 units.；A Chinese white paper describes the 'Embodied Tiangong Ultra' humanoid from Beijing        
Humanoid Robot Innovation Center winning a humanoid‑robot half‑marathon event in 2025, presented as a representative case for embodied intelligence in           
long‑duration autonomous locomotion and operational stability.；Beijing Humanoid Robot Innovation Center, established in November 2023 in the Beijing            
Economic‑Technological Development Area, is presented as China’s first innovation center focused on core technologies, product R&D and application promotion for 
embodied‑intelligence robots, undertaking joint national and local innovation tasks in embodied intelligence algorithms, humanoid bodies, core components and    
system platforms, and promoting pilots in industrial, service and special scenarios.；Chinese reports describe 'Galaxy General' (银河通用) and similar           
humanoid‑robot startups as having raised over USD 300 million, characterized as record‑level financing events in the industry.。[6][4][5][10]                    

现有公开资料显示：Multiple Chinese research and industry reports between 2024 and 2026, including deep‑dive analyses and white papers on embodied intelligence   
and humanoid robots (e.g., from China Academy of Information and Communications Technology), indicate that embodied AI has become a systematic research focus    
with an emerging body of standardized reports and white papers.；Standardization efforts for humanoid robots are reported to involve ISO and IEEE developing a   
general safety standard framework expected to be issued around 2026, with China in parallel piloting industry group standards.；Industry commentary argues that  
embodied intelligence can transform humanoid robots from pre‑programmed tools into 'general labor' capable of autonomous decision‑making in unstructured         
environments, fundamentally reshaping competitive dynamics in the humanoid sector and making early mass production and scenario deployment a key strategic       
advantage.。[3][11][6][7][9]                                                                                                                                     

